  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Active weather pattern through midweek with multiple \nrounds of showers and thunderstorms, including severe thunderstorm \npotential Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nWidespread showers continue across western and north-central NY \nMonday afternoon and evening as a frontal boundary associated with a \nlow over western Ontario continues to slowly progress northeastward \nacross the area. Showers will become more scattered for areas closer \nto Lake Erie Monday afternoon, before another round approaches from \nthe west this evening. This boundary will generally remain over the \nregion through midweek with multiple rounds of showers and storms \npassing overtop of the upper level ridge centered over the southern \nOhio River Valley. \n\nSolutions from hi-res guidance remains very mixed on the progression \nof storms from eastern MI across southwest ON into western NY late \ntonight into early Tuesday morning. However, enhanced flow from a \nweak shortwave passing over the ridge may provide enough shear for \norganized storms with gusty winds across the Niagara Peninsula \neastward. These storms will continue to move southeast toward the \nNY/PA border through mid-morning. Behind this subtle feature, little \nforcing will be present with a low-level cap present, but diurnal \ncirculations may support isolated thunderstorm development along the \nlake breeze boundaries in the afternoon. If any storms can develop, \nthey would occur with ~1500 J/kg of MLCAPE and up to 35 kt of \neffective shear and fairly straight hodographs that will support \nsplitting storms. Additionally, steering flow will be aligned with \nlake breeze boundaries across the Niagara Frontier which is a \nconsideration for excessive rainfall. Storm coverage will increase \nTuesday night with better forcing with upstream convection in \neastern MI and southwest ON. \n\nThe strongest shortwave passing through the area and with the best \npotential for severe thunderstorms looks to be Wednesday as a belt \nof ~50 kt flow at 500mb passes over the ridge. This will provide \nample shear across the lower Great Lakes into portions of the Mid-\nAtlantic for organized thunderstorms capable of producing strong \nwinds and hail. Any lingering morning convection will once again \nlikely impact timing of CI, but favorable upper level support across \na well of 2000+ J/kg CAPE could result in multiple rounds of strong \nstorms in the afternoon as well as overnight into early Thursday \nmorning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and humid through midweek with \"feels-like\" \ntemperatures approaching the low-90s on Wednesday.\n\nAs the upper level ridge from the central CONUS shifts over the \nsoutheast and Mid-Atlantic by midweek, temperatures aloft will begin \nto climb, with 850mb temps around 18-20 degC Wednesday. This would \nbe above the climatological 95th percentile for early September with \nafternoon highs 5-8 degF above normal. Additionally, upper 60s to \nlow 70s dew points will keep western NY fairly uncomfortable \nhumidity wise Tuesday and Wednesday. Overnight lows near 10 degF \nabove normal will also provide little overnight relief.\n\nTypical warmer spots across western NY in the Genesee Valley and \nnorthern Finger Lakes region may tough Heat Advisory criteria \nWednesday afternoon, however duration and coverage is not enough at \nthis time to consider any headlines. Additionally, cloud cover from \nlingering morning storms or developing afternoon storms may provide \nrelief.",
